From 0ebd57ee8013b974450796fe61f77add400de054 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HiGarfield <HiGarfield@126.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 16:23:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] usb: xhci: fix TRB write race behind MT7621 USB3 reset storm

Fixing the downstream symptoms of the USB3 reset storm on MT7621 -
descriptors_changed() misjudging a failed control read as a replaced
device, and XHCI_SPURIOUS_SUCCESS swallowing genuine error events -
leaves the root cause of the resets themselves in place: a missing
write barrier in queue_trb().

queue_trb() writes the 16-byte TRB in four 32-bit chunks, with the
chunk holding the cycle bit (field[3]) written last, but without any
barrier between the data fields and the cycle bit. On weakly ordered
memory systems - such as the MIPS cores used by MT7621 - the stores
are not guaranteed to complete in program order, so the xHC can fetch
a TRB whose cycle bit is valid while the remaining fields still hold
stale data.

A half-written command TRB is the dangerous case. Set TR Dequeue
Pointer, Stop/Reset Endpoint, Configure Endpoint and friends are
queued back to back while the controller is still working through the
previous command; error recovery, device reset and re-enumeration all
do exactly that under load. The controller then executes a command
built from garbage, which shows up as:

    xhci-mtk 1e1c0000.xhci: Mismatch between completed Set TR Deq Ptr
    command & xHCI internal state.

can move an endpoint ring's dequeue pointer to a bogus address, and
can even make the host controller DMA from wild memory (Host System
Error). The endpoint ring is left desynchronized, every following
bulk URB fails, and usb-storage escalates to a full USB device reset.
Each reset re-enumerates the device and queues more commands, so the
race fires again: the self-sustaining reset storm seen on MT7621 under
heavy I/O, ending in blk_update_request I/O errors and an ext4
journal abort. At low load the race window is never hit.

Fix by adding a write barrier before writing the cycle bit, so the
whole TRB is visible to the controller before it may be consumed.
This is equivalent to upstream commit 576667bad341 ("xhci: make sure
TRB is fully written before giving it to the controller"), which was
backported to 4.4 stable as 5eaa0aa8, both long after this tree was
cut. Bulk transfer rings were already protected by the wmb() in
giveback_first_trb() plus the first-TRB cycle-bit gate; the command
ring had no such protection. On x86 wmb() degrades to a compiler
barrier (TSO already orders the stores), so there is no cost there.
---
 drivers/usb/host/xhci-ring.c | 27 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/usb/host/xhci-ring.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/xhci-ring.c
@@ -2885,6 +2885,33 @@ static void queue_trb(struct xhci_hcd *x
 	trb->field[0] = cpu_to_le32(field1);
 	trb->field[1] = cpu_to_le32(field2);
 	trb->field[2] = cpu_to_le32(field3);
+
+	/*
+	 * The controller starts parsing a TRB as soon as its cycle bit is
+	 * set, even if the driver has not finished writing it yet.  The
+	 * chunk holding the cycle bit (field[3]) is written last, but on
+	 * weakly ordered memory systems - such as the MIPS cores in MT7621
+	 * - the stores are not guaranteed to complete in program order, so
+	 * the xHC can fetch a TRB whose cycle bit is valid while the other
+	 * fields still contain stale data.
+	 *
+	 * A half-written command TRB is the dangerous case.  Set TR Dequeue
+	 * Pointer, Stop/Reset Endpoint, Configure Endpoint and friends are
+	 * queued back to back while the controller is still working through
+	 * the previous command; error recovery, device reset and
+	 * re-enumeration all do exactly that under load.  The controller
+	 * then executes a command built from garbage, which shows up as
+	 * "Mismatch between completed Set TR Deq Ptr command & xHCI internal
+	 * state", can move an endpoint ring's dequeue pointer to a bogus
+	 * address, and can even make the host controller DMA from wild
+	 * memory (Host System Error).  The endpoint ring is left
+	 * desynchronized, every following bulk URB fails, and usb-storage
+	 * escalates to a full USB device reset - the reset storm seen on
+	 * MT7621 under heavy I/O.  A write barrier before the cycle bit
+	 * guarantees the whole TRB is visible to the controller before it
+	 * may be consumed.
+	 */
+	wmb();
 	trb->field[3] = cpu_to_le32(field4);
 	inc_enq(xhci, ring, more_trbs_coming);
 }
